7-ZIP
================================================================================

The archive formats, codecs and the extract and update logic come from 7-Zip,
linked statically into the app as Libraries/SevenZipKit/lib7zip_apple.a.

| Component | Version                | License                         | SPDX                         |
|-----------|------------------------|---------------------------------|------------------------------|
| 7-Zip     | 26.04+MacRAR.Patch.5   | GNU LGPL 2.1 or later, with the | LGPL-2.1-or-later AND        |
|           |                        | unRAR restriction for the RAR   | BSD-3-Clause AND             |
|           |                        | decoder; some files BSD 3-/2-   | BSD-2-Clause AND             |
|           |                        | clause or public domain         | LicenseRef-unRAR-restriction |

Copyright (C) 1999-2026 Igor Pavlov. https://www.7-zip.org

The library is a modified build. The fork it comes from adds a C interface and
its build and release scripts in a directory of its own (7zip-apple/), and
changes three of 7-Zip's own files, each of which says so with the date. The C
interface is under 7-Zip's licence, the GNU LGPL 2.1 or later. The changes:
- 2026-10-13: on Apple systems, ZIP names stored in a Windows code page are
  read with the code pages the application gives (CPP/7zip/Archive/Zip/
  ZipItem.cpp, CPP/Common/StringConvert.cpp, CPP/Common/StringConvert.h).

Source:
- Repository:  https://github.com/ARAS-Workspace/7zip (branch apple)
- Release:     https://github.com/ARAS-Workspace/7zip/releases/tag/mac-v26.04%2BMacRAR.Patch.5
- Upstream:    https://github.com/ip7z/7zip, release 26.04

MacRAR's own source is public, so the app can be rebuilt and relinked against a
modified build of the library, as the LGPL asks.

Licence texts, byte for byte as 7-Zip 26.04 ships them:
- 7-Zip's licence (DOC/License.txt): Libraries/SevenZipKit/LICENSE, in the app
  as 7zip-license.txt.
- The GNU LGPL 2.1 it refers to (DOC/copying.txt): Libraries/SevenZipKit/
  copying.txt, in the app as lgpl-2.1.txt.

The unRAR restriction means the RAR decoding code may not be used to develop a
RAR (WinRAR) compatible archiver. The app only decodes RAR archives with it;
creating them is left to RARLAB's own rar program, which the user installs.
